Study Protocol Overview

Research Question: Among newly diagnosed patients with primary lung adenocarcinoma in the TCGA-LUAD cohort, what baseline demographic, smoking behavioral, pathological staging, and surgical margin factors are independently associated with Overall Survival (OS)?
Rationale: Real-world genomic and clinical registries require rigorous baseline risk adjustment to avoid residual confounding when evaluating prognostic markers or observational treatment outcomes.
Study Design: Retrospective observational cohort study.
Target Population: Patients registered in the NCI TCGA-LUAD primary cohort (N=585).   

Inclusion Criteria:
Primary histologically confirmed diagnosis of lung adenocarcinoma.
Complete baseline record in clinical_patient_luad.   

Valid non-negative follow-up or survival time (Follow-up Days≥0).

Exclusion Criteria:

History of prior or synchronous primary malignancy (prior_malignancy == "yes").
Missing vital status or zero total follow-up duration without documented death.
Pathological stage unmapped or unrecorded at diagnosis.

Exposures & Key Predictors:

Primary Exposure: AJCC Pathologic Stage (Stage I, II, III, IV).
Key Covariates: Age at diagnosis (continuous and stratified <65 vs. ≥65), Sex, Smoking History (Ever vs. Never Smoker), Surgical Margin Status (R0 vs. R1 /R2 /RX ).

Primary Outcome:
Overall Survival (OS): Calculated as duration in days from initial diagnosis to death from any cause (event) or last documented live follow-up (censored).
Missing Data Strategy:
Complete-case analysis for multivariable Cox models where covariate missingness <5%.
Categorical missingness indicators or grouped unknown categories for variables with >5% missingness (e.g., residual tumor margins, pack years).   

Statistical Analysis Strategy:
Descriptive characterization (frequencies/percentages for categorical factors; median/IQR or mean/SD for continuous variables).
Kaplan-Meier overall survival estimates with log-rank testing across clinical strata.
Univariable and Multivariable Cox Proportional Hazards modeling reporting Hazard Ratios (HR), 95% Confidence Intervals (95% CI), and Wald p-values.
Model diagnostics using scaled Schoenfeld residuals to test proportional hazards assumptions.
Methodological Limitations: Observational structure without randomized control, unstandardized longitudinal treatment dosage details, potential selection bias inherent to surgical tissue acquisition in TCGA.